<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/advanced/media-law-and-ethics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Everything a journalist publishes is a legal act, and the law of defamation, privacy,
    contempt, data protection and copyright defines the field the reporting must play in.
    Ethics is the layer above the legal floor: the law tells you what you may publish, the
    professional codes ask whether you <em>should</em>, and the two diverge regularly — legal
    is not the same as right. This chapter works through the five areas of law the syllabus
    names, with the working defences and tests a newsroom actually applies, then the ethical
    frameworks that govern everything the law leaves open. The examples below draw mainly on
    English law (the NCTJ tradition), with American contrasts where the difference is
    instructive; principles travel, statutes do not.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="defamation, defences, privacy, sources and secrecy" widget="equationStep" config={lawCfg} />

  <h2>Defamation</h2>
  <p>
    Defamation is the publication of a false statement that lowers a person's reputation in the
    eyes of right-thinking people, or causes them to be shunned or avoided. Written or broadcast
    defamation is <strong>libel</strong>; spoken is <strong>slander</strong> — the practical
    difference being permanence, and libel's reach: a defamatory sentence online is republished
    with every view. To succeed in England and Wales a claimant shows (1) the statement was
    published to a third party, (2) it is defamatory in meaning — including <em>imputations</em>,
    since "X was seen leaving the police station at 2 a.m." defames by implication, and meaning
    can also be by <em>inference from a true juxtaposition of true facts</em> — and (3) serious
    harm (Defamation Act 2013, s.1): for a person, that the statement caused or is likely to
    cause serious harm to reputation; for a company, serious financial loss. In the United States
    the framework inverts for public figures: after <em>New York Times v Sullivan</em> (1964) a
    public official must prove <strong>actual malice</strong> — knowledge of falsity or reckless
    disregard for the truth — which is why American reporting on the powerful is bolder and why
    the case is a landmark of civil-rights-era journalism.
  </p>
  <p>
    The defences are the daily working tools. <strong>Truth</strong> (s.2): the statement is
    substantially true — the ultimate defence, and the reason the verification lesson's
    attribution and evidence habits are legal survival, not just quality control. <strong>Honest
    opinion</strong> (s.3): a genuinely held view on facts that are stated (or widely known) and
    indicated as opinion — "in my view the mayor's plan is unaffordable" is defensible if the
    underlying figures are sound. <strong>Privilege</strong>: fair, accurate and contemporaneous
    reports of courts and Parliament carry absolute or qualified privilege — the public's right
    to know what happens in its institutions, even if the defamatory words turn out false.
    <strong>Public interest</strong> (s.4): the editor reasonably believed publication was in the
    public interest — the descendant of the <em>Reynolds</em> defence, judged by the seriousness
    of the allegation, the source's reliability, and the steps taken to verify and get comment.
    <strong>Offer of amends</strong> (s.5): a prompt, willing correction and apology can be a
    full defence where the error was innocent. Note who bears which burden: in England the
    <em>defendant</em> must prove the defence; in the US after Sullivan the claimant carries most
    of the load. Worked example: "Councillor Bell took £50,000 from the youth fund." Elements:
    published, identifies Bell, imputes theft — defamatory. Paths: prove the taking and its
    criminality (truth); or reframe as documented opinion on an audited discrepancy (honest
    opinion, facts stated); or report the audit committee's finding verbatim the day it is
    published (privilege). Publishing on a rumour with none of the three is the classic
    uninsured position.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "adding 'allegedly' makes a defamatory claim safe." It does
    not. "Allegedly" signals that you know you lack proof, and a meaning that defames is still
    defamatory — the word adds nothing except evidence you suspected the problem. What protects
    you is the substance: evidence, attribution to a privileged proceeding, or an honest opinion
    built on stated true facts.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy and data protection</h2>
  <p>
    Privacy law in England developed through the misuse of private information (after
    <em>Campbell v MGN</em>, 2004) and breach of confidence, interpreted through Article 8 of
    the European Convention on Human Rights (private and family life) balanced against Article
    10 (expression). The working test has two stages: did the person have a
    <strong>reasonable expectation of privacy</strong> in the information — medical records,
    family life, sexual history and home life usually yes; public conduct in a public place,
    usually no — and if so, does the <strong>public interest</strong> in publication justify the
    intrusion? "The public is interested" is not the public interest: a minister's misuse of
    office is public business; a celebrity's entirely private health matter is not, which is why
    the UK press's hospital-stakeout era ended in injunctions. The phone-hacking scandal —
    journalists illegally intercepting voicemail, the public inquiry under Lord Justice Leveson
    (2011–12) — reset this balance and the regulatory landscape: UK newspapers now answer
    chiefly to IPSO (enforcing the Editors' Code) or Impress, and broadcasters to Ofcom.
  </p>
  <p>
    Data protection is the modern half of privacy. The UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018
    require a lawful basis for processing personal data, impose duties on those handling it, and
    carry fines up to £17.5 million or 4% of global turnover. Journalism has a bespoke carve-out:
    the "special purposes" exemption (journalism in the public interest) displaces much of the
    regime where compliance would be incompatible with the journalistic purpose — but it is a
    balancing exemption a regulator or court can weigh, not automatic immunity. Practical
    newsroom rules follow: hold source and story data securely, do not publish private contact
    details or location data that enable harassment (a rising duty in the doxxing era), and
    minimise the personal data collected in crowdsourced verification.
  </p>

  <h2>Contempt of court and reporting restrictions</h2>
  <p>
    Contempt law exists to protect the integrity of trials — the principle that a verdict
    should be reached in the courtroom, not the newspaper. Under the Contempt of Court Act 1981
    the <strong>strict liability rule</strong> applies: once proceedings are <em>active</em>
    (from arrest, or issue of a warrant or charge, until the case is disposed of or abandoned),
    publishing anything that creates a <strong>substantial risk of serious prejudice</strong> to
    the trial is contempt — no intent required, punishable by an unlimited fine and up to two
    years' imprisonment. The classic breach: publishing a suspect's previous convictions or a
    confession-style narrative before trial. Worked example: a man is charged with robbery; a
    paper reports his three prior robbery convictions and "police are certain he is the man".
    Proceedings active, prejudice substantial, defence nil: contempt. The same facts reported
    after conviction are privileged court reporting. Journalists also guard against
    <strong>jigsaw identification</strong> — several individually anodyne details (street, job,
    age) that together identify a person the law has anonymised. Reporting restrictions come in
    defined forms: s.4(2) postponement orders (delay reporting until the trial ends), lifetime
    anonymity for complainants in sexual-offence cases (Sexual Offences (Amendment) Act 1992),
    automatic protections for children in youth proceedings, and open-justice exceptions that
    cut the other way (courts can sit in private only on statutory grounds).
  </p>

  <h2>Copyright</h2>
  <p>
    Copyright protects the expression of ideas — text, photographs, video, music — under the
    Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988, automatically and for life plus 70 years for
    literary works. Journalism lives on both sides of the line. What you <em>use</em>: fair
    dealing permits use for reporting current events, criticism and review, or quotation — but
    the exceptions require <strong>sufficient acknowledgement</strong>, only as much as needed
    for the purpose, and (a trap for aggregators) photographs get no current-events exception in
    the UK. A screengrab of another outlet's exclusive with a credit is not licensing; it is
    infringement with manners. What you <em>own</em>: freelance contracts determine who holds
    rights to commissioned work — a detail that decides whether your investigation can be
    syndicated, and one of the real economic fault-lines of the trade. For the verification
    lesson's reverse-image work, rights-aware practice means locating the original, crediting
    the creator, and using it under a licence or an exception, not lifting it.
  </p>

  <h2>Introduction to court reporting</h2>
  <p>
    Court reporting is where several strands meet, and where precision is a legal requirement.
    The core protection: a <strong>fair, accurate and contemporaneous</strong> report of
    proceedings carries privilege — so the discipline is to report what was said in court, in
    proportion, on the day. Pre-trial (the "charge sheet" phase) only limited facts are safe:
    name, age, address, occupation, the charges, the court and the date — not the prosecution's
    narrative, which is precisely what contempt law fences off. In the courtroom: report
    evidence as evidence ("the prosecution alleges…", "the jury heard…"), distinguish
    allegations from findings, note that a defendant is innocent until convicted, and name the
    verdict and sentence accurately. Certain cases carry their own regimes — youth court
    proceedings cannot generally identify under-18s; sexual-offence complainants are anonymous
    for life; family proceedings are largely private. The craft habit that ties it together:
    every name, charge, figure and plea is checked against the court record before filing,
    because a wrong verdict or a wrong charge is simultaneously an inaccuracy, a contempt risk
    and a defamation action waiting for its claimant.
  </p>

  <h2>Ethics: the layer above the law</h2>
  <p>
    The SPJ Code's four pillars — seek truth and report it; minimise harm; act independently;
    be accountable — and the Editors' Code that IPSO enforces govern what the law leaves free.
    <strong>Minimise harm</strong> is the pillar the statutes barely touch: how victims and
    bereaved people are treated, following suicide-reporting guidance (Samaritans and WHO
    standards, which measurably reduce imitative contagion by avoiding method details and
    sensational framing), protecting children in coverage, and resisting publish-because-we-can.
    <strong>Independence</strong> is tested by gifts, junkets, advertiser pressure and
    conflicts of interest declared in the standfirst, not hidden. <strong>Accountability</strong>
    is the corrections discipline: a prominent, prompt, honest correction is both the ethical
    duty and, as the defamation section showed, the best legal mitigation — corrections pages
    are trust infrastructure. The modern threat on the legal side is the <strong>SLAPP suit</strong>
    — strategic litigation against public participation, where a deep-pocketed subject buries a
    small outlet in legal costs to kill a story regardless of merit; anti-SLAPP statutes in many
    US states and the EU's 2024 anti-SLAPP directive are the institutional response, and their
    uneven coverage maps neatly onto the press-freedom rankings compiled yearly by Reporters
    Without Borders and the CPJ.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Comparative anatomy of a libel regime.</strong> Put the UK and US systems side by
    side and the philosophical split appears: the US treats reputation as lightly weighted
    against public debate (Sullivan, and the rule that public figures bear the falsity burden),
    while pre-2013 England was a claimant's paradise — no serious-harm threshold, defendant
    proves truth, one-year limitation — which produced "libel tourism" and the 2013 Act's
    reforms (serious harm, a statutory public-interest defence, costs protections). The lesson
    is that defamation law is not natural law but a chosen point on a dial between reputation
    and speech, and journalists who publish across jurisdictions face <em>all</em> the points on
    that dial at once: a story legal in New York may be actionable in London. Publishers respond
    with pre-publication review calibrated to the strictest forum their publication reaches.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Platform-era problems are outrunning settled doctrine:
    who is the "publisher" of a defamatory algorithmic snippet or an AI-generated summary, and
    what does "publication to a third party" mean when a syndication feed is instant and global?
    Privacy jurisprudence is being tested by open-justice data — court records are public, but
    searchable, aggregated, permanently indexed public records behave differently from paper
    files. And the SLAPP arms race raises an unresolved design question: how do you immunise
    small publishers without immunising actual falsehoods hiding behind "journalism" labels?
    Watch the case law on all three; this is the most dynamically changing corner of the
    syllabus.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NYT v Sullivan (1964); Defamation Act 2013; Contempt of Court Act 1981; Campbell v MGN (2004); the Leveson Report (2012); SPJ Code of Ethics; Editors' Code (IPSO); RSF Press Freedom Index.</p>
